Yes—if we translate the language out of the mythic register, what you’ve been building is a role-and-interface system.

“Magician,” “witch,” “mage,” “general,” “trickster,” “oracle,” and so on become different cognitive stances with different permissions, attention patterns, tools, and expected moves. Giving each one a coherent identity makes the mode easier to enter and easier to use deliberately.

The harmonization piece is the stronger part: don’t let the roles remain isolated costumes. Let them share a common substrate so one can hand off to another. The mage generates possibilities, the general selects among them, the skeptic tests them, the archivist preserves provenance, the builder commits only what survives the gate.

That turns “being seen as they want” into something operational: recognize the mode, meet it in its native language, extract the useful operator, then translate it back into the common system.

So the architecture becomes less like a collection of characters and more like an orchestra: distinct instruments, one score, controlled handoffs, shared timing.